#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "58966f53-5f83-5c86-8ffe-38ad0dffd2b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12555" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =0,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "e85cfe0b-4ff6-5cce-9384-aeb930843b49" :question/math-academy-id "e-5265" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x-1)^2 + y^2 - \\dfrac{z^2}{4} = -1,$ find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4((x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + y^2 + 1 \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is $\\mathbb R^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124282 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/id #uuid "1fbe37ed-9022-5c44-851c-0e66fe2fb79f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6078" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets \n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{(x-5)^2}{8} + \\dfrac{y^2}{50} + (z-3)^2 = -1\n$$\n\nand the plane $z = 4$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z = 4,$ we substitute $z = 4$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}+(z-3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}+1=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e5f94d8-e377-52d6-8fdd-991054d50053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121478} {:answer/id #uuid "cb910829-dd76-553e-bfa6-5a522e989edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} > 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121479} {:answer/id #uuid "2acfd3c0-352a-50c7-b5bc-6d486c378ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121480} {:answer/id #uuid "5543d032-a1cd-58a6-89b8-c785f98226e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121481} {:answer/id #uuid "66a9fec6-53eb-5991-9279-4e9adc9d22f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} < 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2acfd3c0-352a-50c7-b5bc-6d486c378ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121480} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c16386e-2f29-56bc-80ea-7a1f976538d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cb27705-2ef7-5315-a2e6-466639501b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-164232" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c1f33a3-5a7d-5250-8690-d0b1e2b31503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315589} {:answer/id #uuid "4602829c-92f6-5884-85b8-f5cd2580da68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315590} {:answer/id #uuid "3b48e8e7-5b03-50e7-9a1b-d7300559e1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-2)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315591} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8b79bc-7325-54df-97ca-ff681fa7fd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315592} {:answer/id #uuid "5811de14-eefd-563a-8d85-cd0af5ac3e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4602829c-92f6-5884-85b8-f5cd2580da68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315590} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e1a9ed-4f62-57a1-98dd-46c3510dd0fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b589cdc8-22e6-5881-b7f2-9fb3725db0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164272" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4}=-1,$ find its $z$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ z & =-1\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-3$ and $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9df71cc-f78e-52ed-b8a7-ece95f2f1dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $y$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315596} {:answer/id #uuid "6466b388-768a-5d1d-97c4-04421a3e09e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315597} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad7ab1-8318-54a3-b799-dd5853411793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,3+\\sqrt{2},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315598} {:answer/id #uuid "e769783a-af67-5766-a84a-aab9cdeeea3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315599} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3b18cd-3a99-555d-a721-0ff03d72166b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3-\\sqrt{2},0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6466b388-768a-5d1d-97c4-04421a3e09e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315597} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f501ae1-0a5c-5478-abf3-e669368e0b3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ab5822f-ed35-5736-89b9-c999ed582c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-164277" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=-1,$ find its $y$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(0-3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 4+{y}^{2}-9 & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9952ad4f-cb2e-59dc-a2d1-44bd365bc7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315603} {:answer/id #uuid "ca71480a-c1d0-52e4-b050-182bb9c27c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315604} {:answer/id #uuid "c2249253-9d1a-5655-845e-90f43345d319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315605} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2bebb1-c639-5f4e-b116-fb10f915e7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4-\\sqrt{5},0,0)$ and $(-4+\\sqrt{5},0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315606} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff3da4-e7b1-59f1-85a8-bd35ef00dde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4+\\sqrt{5},0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9952ad4f-cb2e-59dc-a2d1-44bd365bc7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315603} :answer-field/id #uuid "91291ec9-d4e7-571b-83e7-a14536ebcfc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac80c944-b457-5ceb-8e7b-8ad0f749d93d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164278" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1,$ find its $x$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5} & =-1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the hyperboloid does not intersect the $x$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb07d56-3fa3-5046-8d3b-2ecd39381c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $y$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315610} {:answer/id #uuid "bee3f268-6375-5654-b9aa-d74100e20baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315611} {:answer/id #uuid "b90b3c75-ca9e-5fb2-86f8-480b6344bec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6+\\sqrt{5},0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315612} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9b0369-4c6b-539a-98e9-68f48bdf129b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6,0)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315613} {:answer/id #uuid "c2924483-de99-5fd9-8578-e1c1438b78e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6+\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,-6-\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eb07d56-3fa3-5046-8d3b-2ecd39381c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $y$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c03aa5-28e4-5a21-a736-39184c27e49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "754f0d41-0427-55a9-9b96-3f7987630b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-164284" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-(z-1)^{2}=-1,$ find its $y$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-(0-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-1 & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5} & =-3 \\\\ (y+6)^{2} & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the hyperboloid does not intersect the $y$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e920d31d-d868-512e-93de-35bbf89ca31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315617} {:answer/id #uuid "08d0290f-39d9-5388-9902-0bda8462709b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315618} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2f473e-a26c-58b0-8e23-5f9fa69c15b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315619} {:answer/id #uuid "701b5a8f-c4b8-5cd5-8b15-8019b2c65537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315620} {:answer/id #uuid "a344b2d5-7c8c-51c5-940c-9cd4376e0446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d0290f-39d9-5388-9902-0bda8462709b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315618} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f1d285-3a06-59c2-853f-912821471ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c07acc0b-9718-5922-85a6-2aa92546197c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164303" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5aa9d4f-aef4-5c27-8f83-c947cd135b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315624} {:answer/id #uuid "a457dae7-a9ac-560c-be31-22d5c368546a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315625} {:answer/id #uuid "474c85e9-6d21-52c7-9dff-b9ad74f044b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315626} {:answer/id #uuid "099178e4-39cb-532a-8676-55508c397851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315627} {:answer/id #uuid "daee0e03-8b1a-5055-8890-349aafbe2a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5aa9d4f-aef4-5c27-8f83-c947cd135b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315624} :answer-field/id #uuid "916737dc-253b-516e-8eda-5ec6902712f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bb2562b-5a93-5104-a36a-5ac52a24497a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164305" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4fb924-e1aa-52d2-99dc-e07561d57474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} {:answer/id #uuid "36423193-08a3-5c88-ad99-c05b9b732522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315632} {:answer/id #uuid "f262bb87-4871-55d4-ab51-7488c902ae93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315633} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb31a1b-2f6f-502a-a30b-ebc3a5805668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315634} {:answer/id #uuid "9784dae7-59e5-5920-b20e-0b9284653a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}<3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb31a1b-2f6f-502a-a30b-ebc3a5805668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315634} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7cf2916-5bf8-565a-ad20-5bbfc20deef8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e92d8979-8ed2-57ee-bba0-a5ec152cffed" :question/math-academy-id "q-164307" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =(z-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-3)^{2} & =(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\ z & =3\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the circle $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8964de03-c1cd-5577-ba90-8c59cdbea2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315638} {:answer/id #uuid "3f89693d-9a5b-598a-8f97-abaaad3b0288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315639} {:answer/id #uuid "a63a5189-4da2-52e4-b8b4-9aa7a44786e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315640} {:answer/id #uuid "95bb2dd7-8404-5ba1-9fdc-6a088e23f280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315641} {:answer/id #uuid "307b7503-6855-58a0-b270-c50110991a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95bb2dd7-8404-5ba1-9fdc-6a088e23f280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315641} :answer-field/id #uuid "90b26f63-a7cf-5ec9-acb1-f1eab5ef0f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef712763-c7d2-5dcd-9ca9-85b35690019f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164377" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a hyperbola in $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3087b4-b009-5638-b244-ea98ae062981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315645} {:answer/id #uuid "4d61123d-f51c-55c6-8d64-9c05a7741c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315646} {:answer/id #uuid "3019cd0e-f7b8-53ef-a263-a514e67cefea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315647} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3cca58-6c27-5785-a34c-e79e888fc9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315648} {:answer/id #uuid "10d6b8d3-f5da-5828-aece-2039ddea339a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10d6b8d3-f5da-5828-aece-2039ddea339a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315649} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf54bdd-3f28-5615-945b-95c8068938e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2f415cf-02db-5940-b8b7-a0327c22a852" :question/math-academy-id "q-164379" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12}=-1$ and the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=\\sqrt{2},$ we substitute $x=\\sqrt{2}$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-1 \\\\ 2-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a38499c7-55c3-5a9e-8044-d0ea0a672683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294589} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebbd4c0-da35-5e32-b98a-4f35891aef23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294590} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2f1fec-0431-54e9-8542-33a81f075805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294591} {:answer/id #uuid "e377644e-ffd1-5050-9452-9d2ff27b4d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294592} {:answer/id #uuid "ccde8264-6c46-5e45-a78b-6a583bab3808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{6}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a38499c7-55c3-5a9e-8044-d0ea0a672683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294589} :answer-field/id #uuid "94346eb5-262f-588b-bef3-1a091bfda306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d31914a-9f1e-5783-b7aa-a38c819334db" :question/math-academy-id "q-164380" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(z-2)^{2}=-1$ and the plane $z=3$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=3,$ we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(3-2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1 & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $6$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9073e829-bdbc-5fd4-a768-fcfbbf3d1b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121811} {:answer/id #uuid "9dafa6ec-ac28-5d71-bf21-77c972391c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$36$ and $100$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121812} {:answer/id #uuid "a52166f0-2ae6-5669-b785-5da2e7385da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $10\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121813} {:answer/id #uuid "205cbc13-72d2-5d5e-a8b5-659be22e32d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $5\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121814} {:answer/id #uuid "7020ffe6-6830-52d3-b3b3-329346b550a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7020ffe6-6830-52d3-b3b3-329346b550a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121815} :answer-field/id #uuid "4447137c-128f-5cc4-a598-e9a08181bfea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de878af4-60e8-5b69-ad9a-b8c3044d2e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-35410" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e0e436-88ea-59c0-b742-eb516874d076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315652} {:answer/id #uuid "9757b420-9363-5a04-8342-c2c466a90165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{5}-1,0)$ and $(0,\\sqrt{5}+1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315653} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c21ffd-a2f6-5e92-9963-e58b2b6411f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315654} {:answer/id #uuid "d52df98e-a995-5c6d-9d18-8f1c7f489067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,6,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315655} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fd8f1e-c715-55fa-8398-b0b380815b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4,0)$ and $(0,6,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c21ffd-a2f6-5e92-9963-e58b2b6411f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315654} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae3e863-875f-561f-b1c1-8a9d72de5d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "809cd0c2-57c2-514b-bfff-d4849f16397a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35489" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its $y$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-1 & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and $1+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "328f4ff5-ee9a-52d0-8a00-a67b7b452ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 2(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315659} {:answer/id #uuid "d77965d4-1119-5cdc-9c21-fa632a942910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315660} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8c74ef-d0b2-5ead-b119-70491ca7d875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $y=2x\\pm 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315661} {:answer/id #uuid "46ba76ec-cad5-5442-919d-8a0067738604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315662} {:answer/id #uuid "c90b3cd8-2adc-52b6-a0d4-5dffdfad0fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "328f4ff5-ee9a-52d0-8a00-a67b7b452ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 2(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315659} :answer-field/id #uuid "23406618-5755-5060-9855-77059f132ff6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9c71849-32ab-5d76-a9c0-318df90c7d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-71966" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(x-2)^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm 2(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two intersecting lines: $y=2(x-2)$ and $y=-2(x-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f696f675-dc23-56c8-96da-559b9caebb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315666} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b8f82a-19f9-5a1d-a3ab-b201c6c61e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315667} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad25ec-8e65-518d-8cbf-81a0695e08bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315668} {:answer/id #uuid "97094e97-5f62-5d0e-90a9-7fdc5ca0f999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315669} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e6100f-78fa-5964-8f58-0cb8b92b8974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad25ec-8e65-518d-8cbf-81a0695e08bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315668} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f66603-76ab-5b49-ac1a-d07feb79183b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d95c6e25-de35-5f9a-955b-71a91b612bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-74015" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1 & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$"}]}
